installed).

\strong{What each engine represents.}
INLA fits: Gaussian LMM, simple random intercept; GLMM (binomial, Poisson, negative binomial, gamma, beta), simple random effect; Uncorrelated random slope; Factor-by-numeric fixed interaction; Nested / interaction random effects, multi-stratum; Heterogeneous variance by factor level; Heterogeneous residual by factor level; Baseline heteroscedastic MET: iid genotype, iid genotype-by-environment and a sectioned residual; Known-covariance genomic / pedigree random effect; Separable AR1 spatial field; Per-trial separable AR1 field; Grouped temporal AR1 field; Univariate P-spline; Observation weights (Gaussian, identity link); Exact sufficient-statistic aggregation (binomial, Poisson). INLA refuses: Hurdle gamma (zero mass plus a positive gamma part); Correlated random slope; Unstructured genotype-by-environment covariance; Heterogeneous variances with one shared correlation; Factor-analytic genotype-by-environment covariance; Multi-trait covariance; Exact sufficient-statistic aggregation (Gaussian). brms fits: Gaussian LMM, simple random intercept; GLMM (binomial, Poisson, negative binomial, gamma, beta), simple random effect; Hurdle gamma (zero mass plus a positive gamma part); Uncorrelated random slope; Factor-by-numeric fixed interaction; Correlated random slope; Nested / interaction random effects, multi-stratum; Heterogeneous variance by factor level; Unstructured genotype-by-environment covariance; Heterogeneous residual by factor level; Baseline heteroscedastic MET: iid genotype, iid genotype-by-environment and a sectioned residual; Known-covariance genomic / pedigree random effect; Observation weights (Gaussian, identity link); Ridge shrinkage prior on candidate fixed effects; Laplace shrinkage prior on candidate fixed effects; Elastic-net shrinkage prior on candidate fixed effects; Regularised horseshoe shrinkage prior on candidate fixed effects; R2D2 shrinkage prior on candidate fixed effects. brms refuses: Heterogeneous variances with one shared correlation; Factor-analytic genotype-by-environment covariance; Multi-trait covariance; Separable AR1 spatial field; Per-trial separable AR1 field; Grouped temporal AR1 field; Univariate P-spline.
The per-class table is in \code{README.md}, and with a note on each row in
\code{system.file("KNOWN_ISSUES.md", package = "flexyBayes")}.

\strong{Caution (small-group random effects).} Both engines apply the same
default prior, the half-normal on each standard deviation, which the
INLA path represents exactly as an expression prior on the
log-precision, so they agree on the variance component far more
closely than in earlier versions. A model with
very few groups nonetheless carries a weakly-identified variance
component (the data say little about the between-group spread), and
INLA's Laplace approximation is less accurate there than full MCMC.
For a flagship random-intercept model with few groups, pin
\code{backend = "brms"} or supply an explicit informative prior (a
half-Cauchy, per Gelman 2006; see the \emph{priors and regularisation}
vignette).

\strong{Convergence.} The brms fit emits a warning when the sampler may
not have converged (a parameter with Rhat at or above 1.1, or a low
effective sample size). Treat such a posterior with caution ---
increase \code{warmup} / \code{n_samples}, simplify the model, or supply a more
informative prior --- and inspect the full diagnostics with
\code{\link[=summary]{summary()}}. Silence the warning (for intentionally short fits) via
\code{options(flexyBayes.silence_convergence_warning = TRUE)}. The INLA
path is deterministic and carries no such warning.

\strong{Code inspection.} brms is the only engine with a code slot, so
\code{return_code = TRUE} / \code{review_code = TRUE} under \code{"auto"} resolve to
brms --- but only when brms can represent the model. Where it cannot,
the call refuses with \code{auto_no_active_route} rather than returning
the code for a different model. \code{backend = "inla"} with
\code{return_code = TRUE} returns the INLA formula, family and
hyperparameter list; with \code{review_code = TRUE} it refuses until the
INLA-side code slot lands.}

\item{aggregate}{One of \code{"auto"} (default), \code{TRUE}, or \code{FALSE}.
Exact sufficient-statistics aggregation gate. INLA is the only
engine with an aggregated emit. \code{"auto"} consults the aggregation
plan and routes a binomial-logit or poisson-log model through the
per-cell path when the IR is in scope (fixed plus random intercept;
productive compression) and the model reaches INLA. A Gaussian model
is fitted per row: the aggregated Gaussian route cannot yet carry the
residual prior, so \code{TRUE} on a Gaussian model refuses by name
(\code{aggregation_residual_prior_not_composed}). \code{TRUE} also raises a
structured refusal when the plan declares ineligibility, or when no
active engine offers an aggregated route. \code{FALSE} skips the gate
entirely. Aggregated fits carry \verb{$exactness == "aggregated_exact"},
the dispatch trace's \code{path} slot reads \code{"aggregated_count"}, and
their summary reports no marginal likelihood
(\code{aggregated_evidence_unavailable}). For out-of-core datasets that
do not fit in memory, see \code{\link[=flexybayes_stream]{flexybayes_stream()}}.}

\item{plan}{Logical: if \code{TRUE}, short-circuit after intermediate
representation (IR) build and return a \verb{<fb_plan>} object
carrying the IR, the routing decision, the representation plan,
the aggregation plan, and the prediction plan, \emph{without} emitting
backend code or fitting.  Equivalent to calling \code{\link[=fb_plan]{fb_plan()}} with
the same formula triple --- exposed inline as \code{flexybayes(plan = TRUE)} so users can reach the planning object without re-typing
the call.  Default \code{FALSE} preserves the run-
immediately semantics.  Mutually exclusive with \code{return_code} and
\code{review_code}.}

\item{syntax}{One of \code{"auto"} (default), \code{"asreml"}, or \code{"brms"}.
Selects how \code{fixed} is interpreted. \code{"auto"} detects the grammar
from the call shape (a bar-grouped formula is read as brms,
otherwise ASReml); the other values force a grammar.}

\item{known_variance}{Optional numeric vector of length N: a known
variance for each response, added to the estimated residual variance,
\code{Var(y_i) = sigma^2 + v_i}. This is the second stage of a two-stage
analysis, where each response is a first-stage estimate (a variety
mean in one trial, say) and \code{v_i} is the variance of that estimate.
\code{weights} is a different model, \code{Var(y_i) = sigma^2 / w_i}; given
both, the residual variance is \code{sigma^2 / w_i + v_i}. Lowered for
\code{family = "gaussian"} with the identity link: brms fits
\code{y | se(sqrt(v), sigma = TRUE)}, and INLA a row effect with known
variance \code{v_i}. The summaries report \code{sigma} as the estimated part,
and fitted values on both engines leave the known part out. A vector
of zeros fits the model without it. Any other family refuses with
\code{known_variance_not_supported}, and a vector that is not one finite,
non-negative value per row with \code{known_variance_invalid}.
\code{\link[=fb_select]{fb_select()}} carries it into the fold covariance under
\code{covariance = "base_model"}.}

\item{fixed_alias}{\code{"refuse"} (the default) or \code{"drop"}: what to do with
a fixed-effect column that is a linear combination of the columns
before it on the rows with a response, as in a factorial with empty
cells. INLA and brms would report such a column at its prior, so by
default the fit refuses with \code{fixed_effects_aliased} and names the
columns. \code{"drop"} drops them as asreml 4.2 does, on INLA: terms are
taken in model order, and within a term by descending factor levels,
the term's first factor varying slowest; a column that is a linear
combination of the columns before it is fixed at zero. \code{summary()}
reports it with estimate 0 and no standard error, the columns are
listed in \code{fit$extras$fixed_alias$dropped}, and a prediction whose row
is not a combination of the fitting rows is \code{NA} with status
\code{"Aliased"} (see \code{\link[=predict.flexybayes_inla]{predict.flexybayes_inla()}}). \code{\link[=fb_select]{fb_select()}}'s
\code{protected_alias} takes the same values and applies the same rule on
each fold.}

\item{rcov}{Defunct in flexyBayes 0.9.0. This was the ASReml 3 name for
the residual-structure argument; ASReml 4 renamed it to \code{residual} and
flexyBayes follows the ASReml 4 name. Supplying \code{rcov} now raises an
error -- use \code{residual} instead.}
}
\value{
An object of class \code{"flexybayes"}, a list with three
components.

\describe{
\item{\verb{$glm}}{A GLM-compatible object (class \code{c("flexybayes_glm", "glm", "lm")}) with posterior mean coefficients, vcov, residuals, fitted values,
etc. Works with \code{summary()}, \code{emmeans()} and
\code{marginaleffects()}.}
\item{\verb{$brms} or \verb{$inla}}{The native backend object --- a live \code{brmsfit}
on the brms path, or INLA's own fitted object on the INLA path. Use
with \code{bayesplot}, \code{posterior::as_draws()}, and each engine's own
accessors.}
\item{\verb{$extras}}{Additional outputs: posterior \code{summary}, \code{convergence}
diagnostics, \code{variance_comps}, \code{blups}, \code{predictions}, generated \code{code},
\code{param_names}, \code{parse_info}, \code{call_info}, \code{run_time}, \code{model_info},
and \code{reproducibility}: the R, flexyBayes and engine versions, the
thread settings and the seed the fit ran with, which \code{print()} shows.}
}

If \code{return_code = TRUE}, returns the generated backend code instead: a
character string holding the Stan program on the brms path, or a list of
the formula, family and hyperparameter specification on the INLA path.
}
\description{
Specify mixed models using ASReml formula syntax and estimate them on one
of two active engines: INLA (integrated nested Laplace approximation) or
brms (the Stan passthrough). Returns a three-part result: a
GLM-compatible object for use with standard R packages (emmeans,
marginaleffects, etc.), the native backend object for engine-specific
diagnostics, and extras for secondary analyses.
}
\section{INLA threads}{

An INLA fit runs on one thread, \code{num.threads = "1:1"}, whatever
\code{INLA::inla.setOption()} holds. With more threads, identical calls can
return different variance components where the data inform a variance only
weakly. On a 48-trial barley model with 22 localities, eight identical fits
at \code{"4:1"} gave the locality variance from 0.58 to 42.3, and at \code{"1:1"}
from 0.75 to 0.79, with the fixed effects unchanged. Set
\code{options(flexyBayes.inla_threads = "4:1")} for another plan, or \code{"inla"}
for INLA's own default; the plan a fit ran with is in
\verb{$extras$reproducibility}. Several fits at once, as \code{\link[=fb_select]{fb_select()}} runs its
folds, use the cores without this cost.
}

\section{Fitting in a separate process}{

\code{options(flexyBayes.isolation = "process")} runs the engine fit in a
separate R process started through callr, which is in Suggests; without
callr the request refuses with
\code{isolation_requires_callr} before anything is fitted. The option applies
to \code{flexybayes()}, \code{fb_brms()}, \code{fb_inla()}, \code{proceed()} and each fold of
\code{\link[=fb_select]{fb_select()}}. The default, \code{"none"}, fits in this session.

If the operating system kills that process, or compiled code crashes inside
it, the call refuses with \code{isolated_process_died}, naming the engine, the
function called, and the exit status or signal, and this session carries on.
A failure inside the fit, a refusal for instance, arrives exactly as it would
without the option. The call is not retried.

The separate process loads the flexyBayes code this session loaded and
starts from this session's options, INLA settings, environment variables
and random-number state, so it fits the same model with the same seed,
and the random-number stream moves on as it would have. Messages,
warnings and printed output come back in the order they were raised, and
the fit records the process in \verb{$extras$isolation}. A brms fit with a
\code{seed} returns the same draws either way. INLA does not return identical
numbers for two identical calls in one session, and an isolated INLA fit
agrees with an in-process one as closely as two in-process fits agree.
The separate process starts with an empty global environment, so the
formula finds its variables in \code{data}. Starting the process and
moving the data and the fit costs time on every call; see \code{\link[=fb_select]{fb_select()}}
for the measured figures.
}

\section{Deprecated}{

\code{fb()} is deprecated in flexyBayes 0.13.0 and will be removed in a later
release. Call \code{flexybayes()}, which takes the same arguments. A call to
\code{fb()} warns with a condition of class \code{flexybayes_deprecated}.
}

\examples{
\dontrun{
# live fit -- needs an active backend (INLA or brms/Stan)
data(met_example, package = "flexyBayes")
# Simple random intercept model (small budget for example purposes)
fit <- flexybayes(
  fixed  = yield ~ env,
  random = ~ geno,
  data   = met_example$dat,
  n_samples = 100, warmup = 100, chains = 1, verbose = FALSE
)
summary(fit)
coef(fit)
}

}
